    </section><figure class="service-banner"><img src="/images/panel.jpg" alt="Illustration of a home electrical panel" width="1536" height="1024" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Illustrative image</figcaption></figure><section class="pricing-detail" id="pricing"><div class="container"><h2>What will the work cost?</h2><p>Replacing a worn panel takes different work from increasing the power coming into the house. We check the panel, service wiring, grounding, and what you want to add. Then we explain the work and any utility coordination the job needs.</p><a class="button dark" href="/get-help.html?service=panel">Start your panel request →</a></div></section>

    <!-- Content Section -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="content-wrapper">
                <h2 id="why-replace">Why do panels need to be replaced?</h2>
                <p>A panel may need replacement because it has damaged or unreliable equipment, or because it can't accommodate the work you need. Those are different problems. We check the cause before choosing a solution.</p>

                <h3>1. The breakers aren't doing their job</h3>
                <p>A breaker should cut the power when a circuit carries more current than its wiring can handle. If it doesn't, the wiring can overheat and start a fire.</p>
                <p>Federal Pacific Stab-Lok and Zinsco breakers have failed to trip in overload tests. Some Zinsco panels have red or blue breaker handles, but we identify the equipment by its label, not color alone.</p>
                <p>Older fuse boxes need a different check. Oversized or bypassed fuses can leave the wiring unprotected. We'll check for those problems, damage, and whether the box can handle your needs.</p>

                <h3 id="panel-examples">Does your panel look like one of these?</h3>
                <p>These photos can help you identify your panel. Leave the cover in place; we can check the equipment when we visit.</p>
                <div class="panel-examples">
                    <figure class="panel-example">
                        <a class="panel-photo" href="/images/panels/federal-pacific-stab-lok.jpg" aria-label="View larger Federal Pacific Stab-Lok panel photo"><img src="/images/panels/federal-pacific-stab-lok.jpg" alt="Federal Pacific panel with a Stab-Lok label between two rows of breakers" width="960" height="1280" loading="lazy"></a>
                        <figcaption>
                            <h4>Federal Pacific Stab-Lok</h4>
                            <p>Look for the Federal Pacific or Stab-Lok name on the label. If yours matches, talk to us about replacement.</p>
                        </figcaption>
                    </figure>
                    <figure class="panel-example">
                        <a class="panel-photo" href="/images/panels/murray-fuse-box.jpg" aria-label="View larger Murray fuse box photo"><img src="/images/panels/murray-fuse-box.jpg" alt="Older Murray fuse box with four round screw-in fuses below two fuse blocks" width="960" height="1240" loading="lazy"></a>
                        <figcaption>
                            <h4>Older fuse box</h4>
                            <p>This Murray box uses screw-in fuses. Having fuses alone doesn't mean you need a new panel. We'll check its condition and whether it can handle the power you need.</p>
                        </figcaption>
                    </figure>
                </div>

                <h3>2. You're asking more of your electrical system</h3>
                <p>An EV charger, heat pump, or other new equipment can add more demand than your existing service can handle. A breaker that keeps tripping may also point to a problem on one circuit. We'll find out what's causing the problem and calculate how much power your home needs.</p>

                <h3>3. Is a larger service necessary?</h3>
                <p>Replacing a panel doesn't always mean increasing the amount of power coming into your home.</p>
                <p>Intelligent load management can control how much power certain equipment uses at one time. For example, a compatible EV charger can slow down while other appliances are running, then charge faster when more power is available.</p>
                <p>That can sometimes avoid a service upgrade. We'll compare the options for your home before recommending a larger service. Load management won't fix a damaged panel or unreliable breakers.</p>

                <h2>What should you tell us?</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>What changed:</strong> A new appliance, charger, addition, or problem you noticed</li>
                    <li><strong>What happens:</strong> Which breakers trip, what loses power, and when it happens</li>
                    <li><strong>What you know about the panel:</strong> Its label or an issue an inspector identified</li>
                </ul>
                <p>You don't need to remove the cover or diagnose it. A description is enough to start.</p>
                <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/get-help.html?service=panel">Tell us what's happening →</a></p>

                <h2>What does a panel upgrade involve?</h2>
                <p>We check more than the box. The existing wiring, service equipment, and work you're planning determine what needs to change.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>A suitable panel and compatible breakers</li>
                    <li>The grounding and bonding work the installation needs</li>
                    <li>Required disconnects, circuit protection, and surge protection</li>
                    <li>Connections tightened to the manufacturer's specifications</li>
                    <li>Electrical permits, inspections, and utility coordination where needed</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Michigan has separate requirements for residential and other electrical work. We check the rules that apply to your address and project. A list of changes in a newer code edition doesn't mean every existing home needs all of them.</p>
                <p><a href="grounding-and-bonding.html">Read about grounding and bonding →</a></p>

                <h2>What happens next?</h2>
                <p>Tell us what you need. We'll follow up, check whether we need a site visit, and explain the proposed work before you decide. If the job needs a power shutoff, we'll discuss that as part of the plan.</p>
                <p>Adding a charger? Read about <a href="/ev-charger-installation.html">EV charging and load management</a>. Planning backup power? See the <a href="/generators.html">generator options</a>.</p>

                <div class="content-callout warning-box">
                    <h3>Keep the panel cover in place</h3>
                    <p>The main breaker doesn't make every part inside safe to touch. Incoming wires can stay live. If you notice burning, sparking, or heat damage, keep clear and call. If you see fire or smoke, leave the area and call 911.</p>
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

